% Runtime overhead (inline paragraph, no subsection header to save space)

\paragraph{Runtime overhead.}
\label{sec:runtime}
S1 overhead is $+$13.1\% on SciERC, lower on DocRED due to amortization over longer sequences; under LoRA, $+$10.0\% with 37\% lower absolute latency.
Constraint compilation is one-time $<$0.5\,s; scaling benchmarks confirm constant overhead (2--3\%) up to 200 entity types and 1{,}000 relations (Appendix~\ref{app:scalability}).

\paragraph{Beam search compatibility.}
\label{sec:beam-results}
With LoRA, beam search ($k{=}4$)~\cite{anderson2017guided} eliminates all four violation types on SciERC (R-F1 44--46); zero-shot beam achieves DR${=}$0\% on both datasets (CF response is model-dependent; Appendix~\ref{app:beam-search}).
Total latency: 1.54$\times$ (beam 1.32$\times$ $+$ constraint 1.17$\times$; Table~\ref{tab:beam-latency}).

% exp_ids: r16_70b_scierc_zs_s1, r16_70b_docred_zs_s1, 70b_lora_scierc, 70b_lora_docred_200
\paragraph{Preliminary 70B-class validation.}
\label{sec:70b-results}
On Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct (AWQ INT4), zero-shot S1 eliminates DR on both datasets: SciERC 6.41\%\,$\to$\,0\% (R-F1 11.24), DocRED 14.98\%\,$\to$\,0\% (R-F1 13.05); CF rises to 3.50--7.01\%, comparable to 8B zero-shot CF.
% exp_ids: eval_70b_lora_scierc_s1_constrained, eval_70b_lora_docred_200_s1_constrained
LoRA fine-tuning confirms scale-independent composability: SciERC DR 1.50\%\,$\to$\,0\% (R-F1 44.53, $-$0.20\,pp); DocRED (200-document subset; Appendix~\ref{app:power-analysis}) DR 0.22\%\,$\to$\,0\% (R-F1 43.95, $+$0.15\,pp); CF drops from 3.50\% (zero-shot) to 0.18--1.33\% (LoRA), consistent with the 8B pattern (9.90\%\,$\to$\,1.18\%; \S\ref{sec:composability}).

% exp_ids: trie_qwen_ft_scierc, trie_qwen_zs_scierc, trie_llama_ft_scierc, trie_llama_zs_scierc
\paragraph{Comparison with grammar-constrained approaches.}
\label{sec:constrained-baselines}
Our grammar-only ablation (Table~\ref{tab:scierc-ablation}, row~2) implements the same token-level constraints as GCD~\cite{geng2023gcd} and BoostCD~\cite{boostcd2025}---CFG-based JSON structure enforcement plus vocabulary membership masking.
% exp_id: vllm_guided_json_scierc_zs
We verified this with vLLM's \texttt{guided\_json}~\cite{kwon2023vllm}: DR${=}$15.26\%, R-F1${=}$8.75 (Table~\ref{tab:scierc-ablation}, GCD-equiv.\ row), within 0.5\,pp of our grammar-only ablation, confirming identical constraint classes (Appendix~\ref{app:gcd-comparison}).
The ${\sim}$15\% residual DR is the gap our semantic layer addresses (94\% total reduction).
Trie-only constraints without JSON enforcement yield 9--51\% parse failure (Appendix~\ref{app:trie-baseline}).

% Section: Constraint Composition (compressed, 787→~500 words)

At each decoding step $t$, let $G(t) \subseteq V$ and $S(t) \subseteq V$ denote the tokens permitted by the grammar and semantic layers, respectively.
The final mask is their conjunction:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:composition}
  M_{\text{final}}(t) = M_{\text{grammar}}(t) \;\wedge\; M_{\text{semantic}}(t),
\end{equation}
%
where $\wedge$ denotes element-wise AND over the vocabulary $V$; a token is allowed only if it satisfies \emph{both} structural and type-level constraints.
The conjunction is asymmetric: $S(t)$ is defined only over $G(t)$. This reflects the empirical enabling dependency (\S\ref{sec:method}): the semantic layer requires the relation type to belong to $\mathcal{R}$, a property enforced only by the grammar layer.

\paragraph{Grammar-First Fallback.}
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$ (tight constraints leave no token that is both grammar-valid and semantically valid), na\"{\i}ve masking would force selection of a grammar-invalid token, breaking JSON structure.
We enforce a \emph{grammar-first fallback}: semantic masking is skipped and the grammar layer alone controls selection.
Structural integrity is a hard constraint; type violations are soft errors detectable by downstream validation, with DR (ontologically invalid type pair) more severe than CF (individually valid triples with cross-triple type inconsistency).

\paragraph{Constraint Tightness.}
\label{eq:tightness}
We define per-relation constraint tightness $\kappa(r) = 1 - |\sigma(r)|/|\mathcal{E}|^2$ and the schema-level mean $\bar{\kappa} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}|}\sum_{r} \kappa(r)$.
Across three schemas---WikiEvents ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.029$), SciERC ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.333$), DocRED ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.842$)---DR reduction ranges from 94--99\% (low $\bar{\kappa}$) to 42--90\% (high $\bar{\kappa}$), though this correlation is descriptive only ($n{=}3$, $p{=}0.138$); entity type confusability is a critical co-factor (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa-case-study},~\ref{app:tcr}).

\paragraph{Greedy limitation and fallback cascade.}
\label{sec:greedy-limitation}
The semantic layer commits entity types greedily---an instance of Level~3 (non-local) constraints (\S\ref{sec:decidability})---so CF violations arise when the same entity receives different types across triples; beam search mitigates this in favorable configurations (\S\ref{sec:beam-results}).
Grammar-first fallback triggers on 0.5--5.7\% of type-bearing positions under zero-shot decoding, correlating with $\bar{\kappa}$; fine-tuning virtually eliminates fallback ($\leq$\,0.11\%), transitioning the semantic layer from primary enforcer to safety net.
Causal tracing confirms all residual DR occurs exclusively at fallback positions (Appendix~\ref{app:causal-tracing}).

\paragraph{Non-rectangularity and independent enforcement.}
\label{sec:rectangularity}
Type signatures are non-rectangular (0/7 SciERC, 23/96 DocRED rectangular); independent enforcement is a sound over-approximation admitting 33--37\% spurious pairs; fine-tuning eliminates all residual DR (Table~\ref{tab:composability}).

\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Latency per document (SciERC, 551 docs, Qwen3-8B LoRA FT, RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell). Overhead relative to greedy unconstrained.}
\label{tab:beam-latency}
\end{table}

Beam search itself adds 32\% latency (4 beams); constraint overhead increases from 10\% (greedy) to 17\% (beam) because each beam independently maintains grammar and semantic state.
The total 1.54$\times$ overhead is acceptable for offline batch IE; \texttt{early\_stopping} limits additional compute to partial-sequence exploration.

We also explored a type-consensus constraint that forces identical entity types across all relations within a document.
However, CF violations occur precisely where the consensus type conflicts with domain-range constraints, creating a fundamental CF$\leftrightarrow$DR trade-off.
With DR-aware enforcement (skipping consensus when it would violate domain-range), the layer becomes a no-op (100\% skip rate on 551 SciERC documents and 50 DocRED documents).
This confirms that beam search resolves the CF$\leftrightarrow$DR tension through a fundamentally different mechanism: token-level exploration of paths satisfying both constraints simultaneously.

